Note = {4th Euro-Mediterranean Conference for Environmental Integration-EMCEI,
   Sousse, TUNISIA, NOV 01-04, 2022},
Abstract = {Rainfall within the West African region is vital for domestic,
   industrial, agricultural, and socio-economic reasons. Excess or absence
   of sufficient rainfall, therefore, poses a significant threat to the
   region. Several factors, including large-scale oscillations, contribute
   to rainfall intensities in West Africa. Over the years, much attention
   has been paid to the point process and rainfall dynamics, not the
   spatial extent of rainfall. This study uses Mann-Kendall trend analysis
   to study the spatial extent of widespread precipitation over West
   Africa. Widespread rainfall is concerned with the percentage of an area
   within a region covered by a rainfall event. An increase or decrease in
   widespread rainfall over a long period indicates the role of climate
   change within the region. This information can be used to mitigate and
   adapt to the changing climate. In this study, rainfall for the twentieth
   century was obtained from the Inter-Sectoral Impact Model
   Intercomparison Project (ISIMIP) over the West African region (0-15
   degrees N, 18 degrees W-20 degrees E) at a resolution of 0.5 x 0.5
   degrees grid. The spatial extent of daily precipitation was considered
   at thresholds of 1, 5, and 10 mm over ten decades. Results obtained
   showed that the spatial extent of precipitation has vastly been
   decreasing at all thresholds (1901-1910, 1921-1930, 1931-1940,
   1941-1950, 1951-1960, 1961-1970) with increasing trends observed in
   fewer decades (1911-1920, 1970-1980, 1981-1990, 1991-2000). During the
   period, widespread precipitation showed decreasing trends of - 0.007, -
   0.003, and - 0.011 for 1 mm, 5 mm, and 10 mm thresholds, respectively.},
