Abstract = {Learning from existing data allows building models able to classify
   patterns, infer association rules, predict future values in time series
   and much more. Choosing the right features is a vital step of the
   learning process, specially while dealing with high-dimensional spaces.
   Autoencoders (AEs) have shown ability to conduct manifold learning,
   compressing the original feature space without losing useful
   information. However, there is no optimal AE architecture for all
   datasets. In this paper we show how to use evolutionary approaches to
   automate AE architecture configuration. First, a coding to embed the AE
   configuration in a chromosome is proposed. Then, two evolutionary
   alternatives are compared against exhaustive search. The results show
   the great superiority of the evolutionary way.},
