Abstract = {This article discusses neuropsychology within the Spanish-speaking
   would; it reviews its recent history, existing efforts, and future
   directions. It also addresses the practice of neuropsychology with
   Hispanics in the United States and posits that ii should take into
   account the heterogeneity of this population (the largest minority group
   in the country). Hispanic heterogeneity includes, among other variables,
   ethnicity, language, acculturation, age and education. Data from current
   research suggest that these nle relevant variables affecting the
   psychometrics of cognition and therefore, the practice of
   neuropsychology with this population. Suggestions for future research
   are also addressed. (C) 1999 National Academy of Neuropsychology.
